          <p>
            If you landed here from a search for &ldquo;best npm security tool&rdquo; or
            &ldquo;Snyk alternatives,&rdquo; you&rsquo;re probably evaluating a list that includes
            Socket, Snyk, and npm audit. Commit is newer and does something different. This piece
            tells you exactly what each tool measures, where each one wins, and where each one
            fails &mdash; including Commit&rsquo;s genuine gaps.
          </p>
          <p>
            Short answer: most of these tools scan for <em>known</em> vulnerabilities. Commit scans
            for structural risk that exists <em>before</em> a vulnerability is known. They&rsquo;re
            complementary, not substitutes. If you only use one, you have blind spots.
          </p>

          <hr class="essay-divider" />

          <h2>What each tool actually measures</h2>

          <p>
            Before the comparison table, a framing that matters: these tools answer different questions.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>npm audit</strong> answers: &ldquo;Does this package have a reported CVE?&rdquo;
            It submits your <code>package-lock.json</code> to GitHub&rsquo;s Advisory Database and
            returns known matches. Free, built-in, reactive.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Snyk</strong> answers: &ldquo;Does this package have a known vulnerability, and
            can I auto-fix it?&rdquo; Adds license compliance, SAST for your own code, and container
            scanning. Strong database, strong integrations.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Socket</strong> answers: &ldquo;Is this package doing something dangerous <em>right now</em>?&rdquo;
            Static analysis of actual package source &mdash; not just CVE lookups. It catches supply chain
            attacks by scanning newly published version code for suspicious patterns (obfuscated code, unusual
            network calls, environment variable access). That&rsquo;s a different class of detection.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Commit</strong> answers: &ldquo;Is this package a structural single point of
            failure?&rdquo; Behavioral signals &mdash; maintainer depth, release consistency, bus
            factor, contributor history &mdash; from the npm registry and GitHub API. No CVE database.
            No code scanning. Just: how many humans stand between an attacker and the publish button,
            and how consistently have they shown up?
          </p>

